// Testing/BoardIndex.maxon — the check that every row id on `maxon-shv2/PLAN.md`'s SLICE BOARD is
// declared exactly ONCE.
//
// ⭐ **THE BOARD IS A REGISTRY OF NAMES AND NOTHING CHECKED THAT A NAME WAS FREE.** On 2026-08-01 two
// agents filed different rows both called `A2o`, independently, within one hour. Both noticed, both
// renamed to `A2r`, and the collision simply MOVED; a third rename settled it. Three pushes spent on a
// name — and the only thing that ever noticed was a human reading both rows.
//
// ⚠ **THE HALF-CURE ALREADY IN THE TREE IS A DIFFERENT CHECK, AND MEASUREMENT SAYS SO.**
// `scripts/lib/plan-board.sh`'s `row_line_no` refuses a duplicate — but only for the ONE id being
// looked up, and only when `rung-start.sh` or `rung-finish.sh` runs. Both `A2o` rows were FILED while
// nobody was claiming either of them, so nothing looked. Its soft twin `row_line_no_soft` does not
// refuse at all: it answers EMPTY for a duplicated id exactly as it does for a batch member that
// legitimately has no row of its own, and `rung-start.sh` warns once and carries on — so a collision
// among a BATCH's members costs that member its claim rather than stopping anything. **MEASURED when
// this file was written: `X6` named TWO different rows, live on the board, with every script green.**
//
// ⇒ **IT RIDES `spec-test`**, beside `requireLadderIndexComplete` and for its reason: the suite is the
// thing that already runs — dozens of times a day, by every agent, in every worktree. A duplicate filed
// by one agent is then caught by the next agent's next suite run rather than at the next CLAIM, which
// may be days away. A check nobody runs is the drift it exists to catch, one level up.
//
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// WHAT A BOARD ROW IS — two rules that are WRONG, and the tree has already paid for both
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ⛔ **NOT a file-wide "a bolded first table cell is a row id".** PLAN.md's own `A2n` row says so before
// you try it, and MEASURED on this tree that rule finds **23** repeated ids of which **22 are entirely
// legitimate**: every ladder rung (`P1.0a`, `P1.2`, `P1.7a`, `P2.3`, …) appears once in the *Status at a
// glance* index and once in its own detail row, by design. (The row records 19 — the ladder has grown.)
// ⇒ the check has to be SCOPED to the board's own tables, which means finding SECTIONS, not grepping.
//
// ⛔ **NOR an id matched against a CHARACTER CLASS.** The first attempt at this check used one that left
// the `.` out, so it reported the board clean over ids it could not see — blind and green at the same
// time, which is worse than no check at all. It is not a theoretical hole: the board carries eight live
// dotted ids (`R4.1`…`R4.8`). ⇒ **there is no charset anywhere in this file.** The id is whatever the
// first cell says once the bold marks come off, which is how `plan-board.sh` reads it too.
//
// ✅ **THE ANCHOR IS THE TABLE'S OWN HEADER**, which is `plan-board.sh`'s narrowing (3) and its reason:
// a board table is one whose LAST header column is `Claimed (UTC)`. There are exactly two — the BATCH
// table and *The BOARD* — and the header DECLARES the columns, so deriving from it is the one reading
// that cannot drift out from under either reader.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS A SECOND READER OF THAT FORMAT, AND IT IS SAID OUT LOUD RATHER THAN HIDDEN.**
// `scripts/lib/plan-board.sh` is the other, and its own header explains at length why a second copy is
// this project's signature bug. Two things keep this from being one. It reads a strict SUBSET — which
// lines are rows, and what the first cell says; never the column layout, which is precisely where the
// two tables differ and where a mis-parse would be silent. And **both readers REFUSE LOUDLY when the
// anchor stops matching** — `found no board table` there, `read ZERO rows` here — instead of reading
// nothing and reporting all clear. A format change therefore lands as two failures, never as one green.
//
// ⚠ **WHY NOT PUT IT IN `plan-board.sh` INSTEAD, WHERE THERE IS ALREADY A PARSER?** Because that is the
// slot the `A2o` collision proved insufficient: those scripts run once per rung, at CLAIM and at CLOSE,
// and the collision is created at FILING time by an agent doing neither. The gate has to sit on
// something every agent runs constantly, and that is the suite.

// Where the board lives, relative to the repo root. Anchored at the working directory for the reason
// `LadderIndex` and `spec-test`'s own default spec directory are: the suite is invoked from the repo
// root, and a run from anywhere else has already failed to find `specs-shv2`.
let BoardRelativePath = "maxon-shv2/PLAN.md"

// ⭐ **THE ANCHOR.** A board table is one whose LAST header column is this — see the header for why the
// column heading, rather than the section or the row shape, is what the scope is derived from.
let ClaimedColumnHeading = "Claimed (UTC)"

// The two markdown tokens the row rule is spelled in. Named because each is a magic character in a
// format this file is PARSING, and a bare "|" three functions apart is how one rule becomes two.
let TableCellSeparator = "|"
let BoldDelimiter = "**"

// `"| a | b |".split("|")` gives `["", " a ", " b ", ""]`: the empty text AFTER the closing separator is
// the last element, so the last CELL is the one before it. This is that 2 — the count a table line must
// have before it has a last cell at all, and the offset that reaches it. ONE constant, because it is one
// fact about where a row's text stops.
let ClosingCellParts = 2

// How many rows declaring one id make a collision. Two rows is the whole defect; a third is the same
// defect with more claimants.
let DuplicateRowThreshold = 2

// ⛔⛔ **HOW MANY ANCHORED TABLES THE BOARD MUST STILL HAVE, because `rows.isEmpty()` ONLY CATCHES
// TOTAL BLINDNESS AND THE LIKELY EDIT IS PARTIAL** (BATCH10 review). This file's header argues that a
// format change lands as two failures rather than one silent green, and that argument holds for the
// anchor disappearing EVERYWHERE. It does not hold for ONE of the two tables: rename the BATCH table's
// last column, or add a column after it, and this check still reads the BOARD's rows, still finds them
// non-empty, and reports a CLEAN board while no longer looking at the batch ids at all — which is
// precisely the "a blind uniqueness check calls every id unique" failure the empty guard exists for,
// arrived at halfway. `plan-board.sh` does not cover the gap either: its cache is still non-empty from
// the surviving table, so it does not `die`, and the loss only surfaces the day somebody claims one of
// the ids that vanished.
//
// ⇒ The count is checked, not just the rows. TWO because the two tables are ONE namespace — the
// `sharedAcrossTablesDocument` fixture is about exactly that — and a board that legitimately grows a
// THIRD anchored table still passes, while one that silently loses one does not.
let MinimumBoardTables = 2

// A count of anchored tables in one document. Its own name rather than a bare count, because the whole
// point of the constant above is that this number means "how much of the namespace was in view".
typealias BoardTableCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// What one scan of a board document yields: its rows, and HOW MANY anchored tables they came out of.
//
// The two travel together because they are one walk's answer. A separate `boardTableCount(text)` would
// be a SECOND reader of the format this file already argues carefully about having a second reader of —
// and the two could then disagree about where a table opens, which is the one thing neither is allowed
// to be wrong about alone.
type BoardScan
	export let rows as BoardRowArray
	export let tables as BoardTableCount

	static function create(rows BoardRowArray, tables BoardTableCount) returns BoardScan
		return Self{rows: rows, tables: tables}
	end 'create'
end 'BoardScan'

// How deep each list sits under the sentence that introduces it — one depth per nesting, never one
// constant standing for two (see `IndentedReport`'s header). An item under the panic's headline; a
// detail under the item that owns it.
let HeadlineItemIndent = "\n  "
let NestedDetailIndent = "\n      "

// A 1-based line number in the board document. The counter is bumped BEFORE the line is looked at, so 0
// is never a value a `BoardRow` carries; the range opens at 0 because that is where a counter starts,
// not because 0 means anything.
typealias BoardLineNumber = int(0 to u64.max)

// One row of a board table: the id its first cell declares, and the line it is on.
//
// ⭐ **THE LINE IS HALF THE ANSWER.** A duplicate report naming only the id sends the reader to search a
// 1.9 MB file for a string that is, by definition, in it twice. `plan-board.sh`'s own duplicate refusal
// and the error-code registry's both name every claimant's line, for exactly this reason.
type BoardRow
	export let id as String
	export let line as BoardLineNumber

	static function create(id String, line BoardLineNumber) returns BoardRow
		return Self{id: id, line: line}
	end 'create'
end 'BoardRow'

typealias BoardRowArray = Array with BoardRow

// ⭐ **PROVE NO TWO BOARD ROWS SHARE AN ID.** Called ONCE, by the spec suite's parent process — the
// placement `requireLadderIndexComplete` and `requireRunnerAvailable` already use, and for their reason:
// this is a property of the TREE, not of any one test, so there is no test to fail and nothing
// downstream that could act on it.
//
// ⚠ **THE FIXTURES RUN FIRST, AND THAT ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING.** A scanner that has gone blind reports
// every board as unique, so "PLAN.md is clean" is not a result until something has shown that this
// reader can still SEE a collision. Asked the other way round, a blind check reports a clean board and
// its own fixtures never get to say otherwise.
export function requireBoardIdsUnique()
	requireScannerSeesWhatItMust()

	let path = try FilePath.from(BoardRelativePath) otherwise panic("BoardIndex: '{BoardRelativePath}' is not a valid path")
	let text = try File.readText(path) otherwise panic("BoardIndex: could not read {BoardRelativePath} — it carries the SLICE BOARD every parallel agent files and claims a row on. Run the suite from the repo root.")

	let scan = scanBoard(text)
	let rows = scan.rows
	if rows.isEmpty() 'readNothing'
		panic("BoardIndex: read ZERO rows out of {BoardRelativePath}'s board tables. A board table's header ends with a '{ClaimedColumnHeading}' column and its rows carry a bold id in the first cell — reading none of them is this check GOING BLIND rather than the board being clean, and a blind uniqueness check calls every id unique. scripts/lib/plan-board.sh refuses the same way ('found no board table'), and the question is the same one: has the board's column layout changed?")
	end 'readNothing'

	// ⚠ Asked SEPARATELY from the emptiness above, because it is a different failure: that one is this
	// check seeing nothing, this one is it seeing only PART of one namespace and reporting on the whole.
	if scan.tables < MinimumBoardTables 'partlyBlind'
		panic("BoardIndex: found {scan.tables} anchored table(s) in {BoardRelativePath}, and the board is {MinimumBoardTables} — the BATCHES table and the BOARD, which share ONE id namespace. Rows were still read, so this check would have reported a CLEAN board while no longer looking at one table's ids at all. A board table's header ends with a '{ClaimedColumnHeading}' column: one of them no longer does, or a column was added after it. Fix the header, or if the board genuinely has fewer tables now, MinimumBoardTables is the line that says so.")
	end 'partlyBlind'

	let duplicates = duplicateIdReports(rows, source: BoardRelativePath)
	if duplicates.isEmpty() 'everyIdDeclaredOnce'
		return
	end 'everyIdDeclaredOnce'

	// ⚠ **`panic` TAKES A STRING LITERAL** — `panic(msg)` for a `let`-bound String is rejected outright —
	// so the list is rendered as text and INTERPOLATED. The same constraint `LadderIndex`,
	// `HarnessSelfTest` and `ScaleHistorySelfTest` all document.
	let report = indentedList(duplicates, indent: HeadlineItemIndent)
	panic("BoardIndex: {duplicates.count()} row id(s) on {BoardRelativePath}'s SLICE BOARD are declared by more than one row. A row id is how one agent's work is named, claimed, closed and referred to, so two rows under one id is a collision nobody can arbitrate — and it stays invisible until somebody tries to claim one of them. Settle the name on the board first.{report}")
end 'requireBoardIdsUnique'

// Every id declared by more than one row, reported ONCE however many rows carry it, with EVERY row that
// carries it. A report that named the same id twice would be the duplication it is complaining about.
//
// `source` is what the claimant lines are addressed against — the board's path in the live check, the
// fixture's own name below. It is a parameter rather than a constant so nothing in this file can print a
// fixture's line number as though it were a line of PLAN.md (`ScaleHistorySelfTest.FixtureSource`'s
// reason, one file over).
//
// The scan is quadratic in the row count and deliberately so: the board is a hand-written markdown table
// of a couple of hundred rows read once per suite run, and the same shape is what `LadderIndex` already
// uses for the identical question over the ladder index.
function duplicateIdReports(rows BoardRowArray, source String) returns StringArray
	var reported = StringArray.create()
	var duplicates = StringArray.create()

	for row in rows 'eachRow'
		if reported.contains(row.id) 'alreadyNamed'
			continue
		end 'alreadyNamed'

		let claimants = rowsDeclaring(rows, id: row.id, source: source)
		if claimants.count() < DuplicateRowThreshold 'declaredOnce'
			continue
		end 'declaredOnce'

		reported.push(row.id)
		duplicates.push("'{row.id}' is declared by {claimants.count()} rows:{indentedList(claimants, indent: NestedDetailIndent)}")
	end 'eachRow'

	return duplicates
end 'duplicateIdReports'

// Where an id is declared, as `<source>:<line>` — the spelling every tool in this tree points a reader
// at a line with, and the one `plan-board.sh` prints from its own duplicate refusal.
function rowsDeclaring(rows BoardRowArray, id String, source String) returns StringArray
	var claimants = StringArray.create()

	for row in rows 'eachRow'
		if row.id == id 'declaresIt'
			claimants.push("{source}:{row.line}")
		end 'declaresIt'
	end 'eachRow'

	return claimants
end 'rowsDeclaring'

// Every row of every BOARD TABLE in `text`, in file order.
//
// A SEAM that takes TEXT and gives back values, so the fixtures below can hand it a whole document
// without anything reading or writing a file — `ScaleHistorySelfTest`'s shape and its reason.
function scanBoard(text String) returns BoardScan
	var rows = BoardRowArray.create()
	var tables = 0 as BoardTableCount
	var inBoardTable = false
	var lineNumber = 0 as BoardLineNumber

	for rawLine in text.split("\n") 'eachLine'
		lineNumber = lineNumber + 1
		let line = rawLine.trimEnd()

		// The header is tested FIRST and closes nothing. The board's two tables are separated by prose, so
		// the second header arrives while no table is open — but a header IS a `|` line, and asking the row
		// rules first would read it as a row of whatever table was open.
		if isBoardTableHeader(line) 'opensATable'
			// Counted HERE, at the anchor, so the number means "tables whose header still matches" — the
			// question `MinimumBoardTables` asks. Counting tables that produced rows would answer a
			// different one and would call an empty-but-anchored table missing.
			tables = tables + 1
			inBoardTable = true
			continue
		end 'opensATable'

		if not inBoardTable 'outsideEveryBoardTable'
			continue
		end 'outsideEveryBoardTable'

		// The first line that is not a table row CLOSES the table. Without this the bolded first cells in
		// the rest of the file — the ladder's two indexes, the rules table — would all be read as board
		// rows, which is the file-wide rule this check exists not to be.
		if not line.startsWith(TableCellSeparator) 'closesTheTable'
			inBoardTable = false
			continue
		end 'closesTheTable'

		match declaredRowId(line) 'row'
			notARowId then continue
			declared(id) then rows.push(BoardRow.create(id, line: lineNumber))
		end 'row'
	end 'eachLine'

	return BoardScan.create(rows, tables: tables)
end 'scanBoard'

// Is this line the HEADER of a board table — the anchor the whole check is scoped by?
function isBoardTableHeader(line String) returns bool
	if not line.startsWith(TableCellSeparator) 'notATableLine'
		return false
	end 'notATableLine'

	let cells = line.split(TableCellSeparator)
	if cells.count() < ClosingCellParts 'noLastCell'
		return false
	end 'noLastCell'

	let lastCell = try cells.get(cells.count() - ClosingCellParts) otherwise return false
	return lastCell.trim() == ClaimedColumnHeading
end 'isBoardTableHeader'

// What a line contributes to the board: the row id its first cell declares, or nothing.
//
// A union rather than an empty String, because "this line is not a row" and "this row declares an id"
// are different answers and a sentinel would make the caller re-derive the difference — the reason
// `LadderIndex.IndexedRow` is one too.
union DeclaredRowId
	declared(id String)
	notARowId
end 'DeclaredRowId'

// The row rule: a table row whose FIRST CELL IS BOLD. That is what holds out the `|---|---|` separator
// and the board's un-bolded continuation and note rows, and it is `plan-board.sh`'s `^\| \*\*`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS ASKED OF THE TRIMMED CELL, WHICH IS A DELIBERATE DIVERGENCE FROM THAT READER.** The bash one
// matches the RAW line — one pipe, one space, two stars — so `|  **A2z** |`, which a hand edit produces
// without anyone noticing, is not a row to it. Leading space inside a markdown cell is not semantic, and
// for a UNIQUENESS gate the safe direction is to see MORE rows than the claimer does and never fewer: an
// id this check cannot see is an id it reports as unique.
function declaredRowId(line String) returns DeclaredRowId
	// `"| a | b |".split("|")` leads with the empty text before the first separator, so the first CELL is
	// element 1 — the reading `LadderIndex.rowScriptName` and `plan-board.sh` both take.
	let cells = line.split(TableCellSeparator)
	let firstCell = try cells.get(1) otherwise return DeclaredRowId.notARowId

	let cell = firstCell.trim()
	if not cell.startsWith(BoldDelimiter) 'notABoldCell'
		return DeclaredRowId.notARowId
	end 'notABoldCell'

	// ⚠ **NO CHARACTER CLASS. THE ID IS WHATEVER THE CELL SAYS.** See this file's header: the attempt that
	// matched ids against a charset left the `.` out of it and went green over eight `R4.x` rows.
	let id = cell.replace(BoldDelimiter, with: "").trim()
	if id.isEmpty() 'boldButNamesNothing'
		return DeclaredRowId.notARowId
	end 'boldButNamesNothing'

	return DeclaredRowId.declared(id)
end 'declaredRowId'

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// THE POSITIVE CONTROL AND THE FIXTURES
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//
// ⭐⭐ **A UNIQUENESS CHECK'S FAILURE MODE IS SILENCE, AND SILENCE IS WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE WHEN IT PASSES.**
// `LadderIndex` needs no fixtures because it has a positive control built into the question it asks: the
// directory listing. A row rule that went blind there reports thirty-one scripts missing from the index,
// loudly. There is no listing to compare a board against — nothing anywhere else in the tree knows how
// many rows the board has — so a scanner that reads the wrong lines, or none, reports a clean board and
// is believed. That is exactly what happened to the first attempt at this check, whose charset could not
// see a `.`; it did not fail, it PASSED, over ids it never looked at.
//
// ⇒ **THE FIXTURES ARE THE CONTROL THE DIRECTORY LISTING WOULD HAVE BEEN.** They run in process, on
// documents literal in this file, because the seam takes text and gives back values: no sub-process, no
// file, and nothing that could touch PLAN.md itself.
//
// ⚠ **THEY SPELL THE FORMAT OUT INSTEAD OF BUILDING IT FROM THE CONSTANTS ABOVE**, which is
// `ScaleHistorySelfTest`'s rule and its reason: a fixture assembled out of the parser's own constants
// follows the parser wherever it goes, which is a test that agrees with the code by construction. A
// renamed anchor must make these documents genuinely stale, and the CONTROL is what turns that from
// several confusing failures into one clear one.

// The name a fixture goes under. It reaches the reader inside this file's own `<source>:<line>`
// claimants, so it must be obviously NOT a path and obviously not the board.
let FixtureSource = "a BoardIndex fixture"

// How many duplicate reports each fixture below must produce. Every one carries exactly ONE collision,
// so a second report is this check seeing a duplicate its fixture does not have — the false positive
// that the 22 legitimate ladder repeats would produce if the scoping ever broke.
let ExpectedFixtureDuplicates = 1

// ⭐ **PROVE THE SCANNER CAN STILL SEE WHAT IT MUST SEE**, before a clean board is read as a result.
function requireScannerSeesWhatItMust()
	requireControlReadsBack()
	requireUnanchoredTableIsInvisible()
	requireDuplicatesAreReported()
end 'requireScannerSeesWhatItMust'

// The valid fixture board, READ BACK ROW BY ROW — id AND line, not merely "it found some rows".
//
// ⭐ Every row of that document is there to be looked for or looked past, and the read-back is the only
// thing that can tell the two apart:
//   · `R4.7` PRESENT   — an id carrying a `.`, the exact character the first attempt was blind to.
//   · `P1.7a` and `P1.2` ABSENT, both declared TWICE — the ladder's legitimate index/detail repeats,
//     held out by the ANCHOR rather than by anything about the ids, which is the whole scoping thesis.
//   · the rules table's bolded prose cell ABSENT — bold is not the scope, the header is.
//   · the struck row and the empty-id continuation row ABSENT — the un-bolded first cell.
//   · the LINE NUMBERS — a scanner one line out of true still finds every id and still reports a clean
//     board, and would send a reader to the wrong line the day it did not.
//   · the TABLE COUNT — the control holds FIVE tables of which exactly two are anchored, so a counter
//     that counted tables rather than ANCHORED ones reads 5 here and would then satisfy
//     `MinimumBoardTables` on a board that had lost both of its real ones. The live check can only
//     refuse when the count is too LOW, so over-counting is the direction nothing else would catch.
function requireControlReadsBack()
	let scan = scanBoard(controlDocument())
	let rows = scan.rows

	if scan.tables != MinimumBoardTables 'wrongTableCount'
		panic("BoardIndex: the CONTROL fixture board carries exactly {MinimumBoardTables} ANCHORED tables among the five it declares, and this scanner counted {scan.tables}. The live check refuses a board with too few, so a scanner that over-counts would pass a PLAN.md whose real board tables had both stopped matching the anchor.")
	end 'wrongTableCount'

	var actual = StringArray.create()
	for row in rows 'eachRow'
		actual.push("{row.id}@{row.line}")
	end 'eachRow'

	let expected = controlRowsAsWritten()
	if not sameList(actual, expected: expected) 'readSomethingElse'
		panic("BoardIndex: the CONTROL fixture board did not read back as it is written. Until it does, a duplicate fixture that fired proves nothing about the rule it names and a clean PLAN.md proves nothing at all — the board's format has moved and this reader has not.\n  it read:{indentedList(actual, indent: NestedDetailIndent)}\n  and the document declares:{indentedList(expected, indent: NestedDetailIndent)}")
	end 'readSomethingElse'

	let duplicates = duplicateIdReports(rows, source: FixtureSource)
	if duplicates.isEmpty() 'noFalsePositive'
		return
	end 'noFalsePositive'

	panic("BoardIndex: the CONTROL fixture board declares every board id exactly once, and this check reported {duplicates.count()} collision(s) in it. A gate that refuses a correct board is worse than the silence it replaces — every rung would have to route around it.{indentedList(duplicates, indent: HeadlineItemIndent)}")
end 'requireControlReadsBack'

// The rows the control document declares, in file order, as `<id>@<line>`.
function controlRowsAsWritten() returns StringArray
	return ["BATCH1@27", "BATCH2@28", "A2n@34", "R4.7@35", "X6@38"]
end 'controlRowsAsWritten'

// Two lists of text, compared element by element.
//
// ⚠ **NOT `Array.equals`, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN TASTE.** `stdlib/Array.maxon:648` compares the
// managed buffer's raw BYTES, which for a `String` element are the handles and not the text: two arrays
// holding the same words compare UNEQUAL unless they hold the same String objects. Measured on this tree
// — two arrays each built by pushing `"alpha"` answer `equals=false`. This control would then fail for a
// reason that has nothing to do with the board.
function sameList(actual StringArray, expected StringArray) returns bool
	if actual.count() != expected.count() 'differentLength'
		return false
	end 'differentLength'

	for (iter, item) in actual.withIterator() 'eachItem'
		let want = try expected.get(iter.index()) otherwise return false
		if item != want 'differs'
			return false
		end 'differs'
	end 'eachItem'

	return true
end 'sameList'

// A table that looks exactly like a board table except that its last header column is not the anchor —
// and it declares one id twice.
//
// ⭐ This is the check's SCOPE, asked from the other side. The control proves the ladder's repeats are
// not reported; this proves WHY — the anchor, and nothing about the ids or the bold rule. It is also the
// state the live refusal exists for: an anchor that stops matching reads ZERO rows, which is what
// `requireBoardIdsUnique` turns into a panic rather than into a clean bill of health.
function requireUnanchoredTableIsInvisible()
	let rows = scanBoard(unanchoredTableDocument()).rows
	if rows.isEmpty() 'invisible'
		return
	end 'invisible'

	var seen = StringArray.create()
	for row in rows 'eachRow'
		seen.push("'{row.id}' at line {row.line}")
	end 'eachRow'

	panic("BoardIndex: a table whose last header column is NOT '{ClaimedColumnHeading}' was read as a board table. The scope would then be the whole file, which reports all 22 of the ladder's legitimate index/detail repeats as collisions — the rule PLAN.md's own A2n row records having already paid for.{indentedList(seen, indent: HeadlineItemIndent)}")
end 'requireUnanchoredTableIsInvisible'

// ⭐ **AND THAT THE REFUSAL STILL FIRES ON A DOCUMENT WRITTEN TO TRIP IT.** Every fixture is reported
// TOGETHER rather than stopping at the first: one edit to the row rule breaks all of them, and a report
// naming one sends the reader back for another sweep per remaining fixture.
function requireDuplicatesAreReported()
	let fixtures = duplicateFixtures()
	if fixtures.isEmpty() 'noFixtures'
		panic("BoardIndex: not one duplicate fixture was checked, so the refusal above has never been observed to fire. A refusal nothing runs is a claim, not a door.")
	end 'noFixtures'

	var broken = StringArray.create()
	for fixture in fixtures 'eachFixture'
		recordFixtureFailure(broken, fixture: fixture)
	end 'eachFixture'

	if broken.isEmpty() 'everyFixtureFired'
		return
	end 'everyFixtureFired'

	let report = indentedList(broken, indent: HeadlineItemIndent)
	panic("BoardIndex: {broken.count()} of {fixtures.count()} duplicate-id fixtures were not reported the way this check says they must be. A refusal nothing runs is a claim, not a door.{report}")
end 'requireDuplicatesAreReported'

// One fixture's verdict, appended to `broken` when it is not what the fixture says it must be.
//
// TWO conditions, and both are necessary. The COUNT alone would be satisfied by a report about the wrong
// id, and the TEXT alone would be satisfied by a check that also invented a collision the document does
// not contain — which is the false positive that makes a gate get routed around.
function recordFixtureFailure(broken StringArray, fixture BoardFixture)
	if fixture.reported.count() != ExpectedFixtureDuplicates 'wrongCount'
		broken.push("'{fixture.what}' carries exactly {ExpectedFixtureDuplicates} collision and this check reported {fixture.reported.count()}:{indentedList(fixture.reported, indent: NestedDetailIndent)}")
		return
	end 'wrongCount'

	let only = try fixture.reported.get(0) otherwise panic("BoardIndex: the '{fixture.what}' fixture reported {ExpectedFixtureDuplicates} collision but element 0 could not be read")

	var missing = StringArray.create()
	for text in fixture.expected 'eachExpectation'
		if not only.contains(text) 'absent'
			missing.push(text)
		end 'absent'
	end 'eachExpectation'

	if missing.isEmpty() 'saidEverything'
		return
	end 'saidEverything'

	broken.push("'{fixture.what}' was reported, but the report is missing:{indentedList(missing, indent: NestedDetailIndent)}\n      and it said: {only}")
end 'recordFixtureFailure'

// What one fixture is: the collision it is about, the text its report must carry, and what this check
// actually said when handed the document. The outcome is computed when the list is BUILT, so every
// fixture runs even when an earlier one has already failed and the report names all of them.
type BoardFixture
	export let what as String
	export let expected as StringArray
	export let reported as StringArray

	static function create(what String, expected StringArray, reported StringArray) returns BoardFixture
		return Self{what: what, expected: expected, reported: reported}
	end 'create'
end 'BoardFixture'

typealias BoardFixtureArray = Array with BoardFixture

// The documents written to trip the refusal, each differing from a clean board by exactly one collision.
//
// ⭐ The FIRST is the one this file exists over twice: the id it duplicates carries a `.`, so a check
// that has grown a character class again fires here and nowhere else.
function duplicateFixtures() returns BoardFixtureArray
	var fixtures = BoardFixtureArray.create()

	fixtures.push(BoardFixture.create("the DOTTED id declared twice in one board table", expected: ["'R4.7'", "{FixtureSource}:5", "{FixtureSource}:7"], reported: duplicateIdReports(scanBoard(dottedIdTwiceDocument()).rows, source: FixtureSource)))

	fixtures.push(BoardFixture.create("the id shared by the BATCH table and the BOARD", expected: ["'X6'", "{FixtureSource}:5", "{FixtureSource}:9"], reported: duplicateIdReports(scanBoard(sharedAcrossTablesDocument()).rows, source: FixtureSource)))

	return fixtures
end 'duplicateFixtures'

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The documents. One `append` per LINE, numbered, because every expectation above is a line number.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// A board with every shape the real one has: two unanchored ladder tables that repeat their rungs, a
// rules table whose first cells are bold prose, and the two anchored tables with a dotted id, a struck
// row and a continuation row among their rows.
function controlDocument() returns String
	var doc = ""
	doc.append("# a BoardIndex fixture — the shapes the real board has\n")  // 1
	doc.append("\n")  // 2
	doc.append("## Status at a glance\n")  // 3
	doc.append("\n")  // 4
	doc.append("| rung | what it is | state |\n")  // 5
	doc.append("|---|---|---|\n")  // 6
	doc.append("| **P1.7a** | Array | DONE |\n")  // 7
	doc.append("| **P1.2** | String | DONE |\n")  // 8
	doc.append("\n")  // 9
	doc.append("## The ladder\n")  // 10
	doc.append("\n")  // 11
	doc.append("| rung | detail | state |\n")  // 12
	doc.append("|---|---|---|\n")  // 13
	doc.append("| **P1.7a** | the SAME rung again, in its own detail row, by design | DONE |\n")  // 14
	doc.append("| **P1.2** | likewise | DONE |\n")  // 15
	doc.append("\n")  // 16
	doc.append("### The rules\n")  // 17
	doc.append("\n")  // 18
	doc.append("| rule | what it means |\n")  // 19
	doc.append("|---|---|\n")  // 20
	doc.append("| **Claim the BATCH row, not its members** | a bolded first cell that is not an id at all |\n")  // 21
	doc.append("\n")  // 22
	doc.append("### The BATCHES\n")  // 23
	doc.append("\n")  // 24
	doc.append("| Batch | Members | Lanes it claims | Why together | Status | Owner (branch) | Claimed (UTC) |\n")  // 25
	doc.append("|---|---|---|---|:--:|---|---|\n")  // 26
	doc.append("| **BATCH1** | `A2n` `R4.7` | L-testing | one thesis | CLAIMED | `slice/one` | 2026-01-01T00:00Z |\n")  // 27
	doc.append("| **BATCH2** | `X6` | L-goldens | another | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 28
	doc.append("\n")  // 29
	doc.append("### The BOARD\n")  // 30
	doc.append("\n")  // 31
	doc.append("| id | Slice | Lane | Status | Owner (branch) | Claimed (UTC) |\n")  // 32
	doc.append("|---|---|:--:|---|---|\n")  // 33
	doc.append("| **A2n** | a row | L-testing | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 34
	doc.append("| **R4.7** | a row whose id carries a `.` | L-stdlib | DONE | `slice/r47` | 2026-01-02T00:00Z |\n")  // 35
	doc.append("| ~~**A2d**~~ | a struck row: its first cell is not bold | L-bootstrap | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 36
	doc.append("|  | a continuation row, whose id cell is empty | | | | |\n")  // 37
	doc.append("| **X6** | the last row | L-ir | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 38
	doc.append("\n")  // 39
	doc.append("prose after the board, which closes it.\n")  // 40
	return doc
end 'controlDocument'

// One board table declaring `R4.7` twice, with an unrelated row between them so the report has to name
// the two lines that collide rather than "the last two rows".
function dottedIdTwiceDocument() returns String
	var doc = ""
	doc.append("# a BoardIndex fixture\n")  // 1
	doc.append("\n")  // 2
	doc.append("| id | Slice | Lane | Status | Owner (branch) | Claimed (UTC) |\n")  // 3
	doc.append("|---|---|:--:|---|---|\n")  // 4
	doc.append("| **R4.7** | the row that was already there | L-stdlib | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 5
	doc.append("| **A2n** | an unrelated row between the two | L-testing | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 6
	doc.append("| **R4.7** | a DIFFERENT slice, filed under a name that was taken | L-ir | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 7
	return doc
end 'dottedIdTwiceDocument'

// The BATCH table and the BOARD declaring the same id.
//
// ⭐ The two tables are ONE namespace and that is not a stylistic choice: `plan-board.sh` scans both into
// a single cache and looks a claimed id up across it, so an id in both is exactly the state its own
// duplicate refusal fires on.
function sharedAcrossTablesDocument() returns String
	var doc = ""
	doc.append("# a BoardIndex fixture\n")  // 1
	doc.append("\n")  // 2
	doc.append("| Batch | Members | Lanes it claims | Why together | Status | Owner (branch) | Claimed (UTC) |\n")  // 3
	doc.append("|---|---|---|---|:--:|---|---|\n")  // 4
	doc.append("| **X6** | `A2n` | L-ir | one thesis | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 5
	doc.append("\n")  // 6
	doc.append("| id | Slice | Lane | Status | Owner (branch) | Claimed (UTC) |\n")  // 7
	doc.append("|---|---|:--:|---|---|\n")  // 8
	doc.append("| **X6** | a board row filed under the batch's name | L-goldens | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 9
	return doc
end 'sharedAcrossTablesDocument'

// A table with the board's exact columns and a duplicated id, whose last header column is NOT the anchor.
function unanchoredTableDocument() returns String
	var doc = ""
	doc.append("# a BoardIndex fixture\n")  // 1
	doc.append("\n")  // 2
	doc.append("| id | Slice | Lane | Status | Owner (branch) | Taken (UTC) |\n")  // 3
	doc.append("|---|---|:--:|---|---|\n")  // 4
	doc.append("| **X6** | a row in a table this check must not read | L-ir | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 5
	doc.append("| **X6** | its twin, which it must not report | L-ir | FREE | — | — |\n")  // 6
	return doc
end 'unanchoredTableDocument'
